WEBVTT

00:00.900 --> 00:08.592
Epidemic and Vaccination. Why vaccinating part of the class
also protects those who were not vaccinated.

00:10.792 --> 00:15.478
This simulator shows how a disease spreads
through a population.

00:15.528 --> 00:21.027
In blue, those who can still catch the
disease. In red, those who are sick.

00:21.077 --> 00:25.022
In green, those who have recovered and are now
immune.

00:25.872 --> 00:32.870
I start with the flu: each sick person passes the disease
to three people and stays sick for a week.

00:32.920 --> 00:39.213
Without a vaccine, at the peak, thirty percent
of the population is sick at the same time.

00:39.263 --> 00:42.807
In the end, ninety-four percent caught the
disease.

00:43.657 --> 00:48.862
Now I vaccinate thirty percent of the
population, before the first case.

00:48.912 --> 00:51.636
The band at the top is the vaccinated.

00:51.686 --> 00:56.088
The peak drops from thirty to twelve percent
and comes later.

00:56.138 --> 01:00.613
And the total number of sick people drops
below sixty percent.

01:01.463 --> 01:03.727
I keep raising the vaccination.

01:03.777 --> 01:07.386
With half vaccinated, the peak almost
disappears.

01:07.436 --> 01:12.239
Past sixty-seven percent, the message turns
green: herd immunity.

01:12.289 --> 01:18.884
The disease finds no path to spread, and those
who were not vaccinated are protected too.

01:19.734 --> 01:25.427
And what if the disease is measles? Each sick
person infects twelve others.

01:25.477 --> 01:30.480
With the same seventy percent vaccinated, the
epidemic comes back.

01:30.530 --> 01:34.232
For measles, the threshold is ninety-two
percent.

01:34.282 --> 01:38.903
That is why measles vaccination has to reach
almost everyone.

01:39.753 --> 01:42.212
Further down, the math behind it.

01:42.262 --> 01:50.121
Three short equations that a high school student can follow,
with the numbers from the current controls.

01:50.171 --> 01:54.152
And the threshold calculation: one minus one
over R₀.

01:54.202 --> 01:56.585
For measles, ninety-two percent.

01:57.435 --> 02:02.996
This simulator came from a plain-language
request, which is right here below.

02:03.046 --> 02:05.547
But the AI can get a formula wrong.

02:05.597 --> 02:11.814
Before taking it to the class, check the
numbers against what you know of the subject.

02:11.864 --> 02:15.166
The one who checks the science is the teacher.
